            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Literary Works</strong><br>\nAs a writer, Sartre produced influential works such as \"Nausea,\" \"No Exit,\" and \"The Roads to Freedom\" series. His literature often portrayed his philosophical ideas about freedom, existence, and human emotion. \"Nausea\" particularly dealt with existential angst and the absurdity of life, while \"No Exit\" introduced the famous line, \"Hell is other people.\"</p>\n\n<p><strong>Political Activism</strong><br>\nSartre was actively involved in politics throughout his life. He was a vocal opponent of French colonialism, particularly in Algeria. During World War II, he was part of the French resistance against the Nazi occupation. After the war, his political focus included advocating for Marxist principles and critiquing American imperialism during the Vietnam War.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Relationship with Simone de Beauvoir</strong><br>\nSimone de Beauvoir, a prominent French existentialist philosopher and feminist, was Sartre's lifelong partner. Their relationship was unconventional; they never married and allowed each other to have relationships with other people. Together, they influenced each other's work and contributed to feminist and existentialist thought.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
