          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Sartre's Philosophy of Existence</strong><br>\nFor Sartre, existence was a project, constantly shaped by the choices one makes. He believed that through every decision, individuals create themselves and their view of the world. His philosophy also tackled the idea of \"bad faith,\" where people deny their freedom and responsibility by conforming to social roles and expectations.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Later Years and Legacy</strong><br>\nIn his later years, Sartre's work turned more towards politics and the study of Marxism, which he attempted to reconcile with existentialism. Despite declining the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1964, his influence extended across philosophical and literary domains. Sartre died on April 15, 1980, leaving behind a rich legacy as a leading figure in existentialist thought.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Sartre's Influence on Modern Thought</strong><br>\nSartre's existentialism has profoundly impacted modern philosophy, psychology, literature, and even pop culture. His ideas about human freedom, authenticity, and the absurd continue to inspire debates and reflections on the human condition. His works are still studied for their rich exploration of human freedom and the limitations imposed by society.</p>\n"
              },
